      "body": "## Overview\n\nKeep the Qwen3.8-27B TP1 GDN `in_proj_ba` MXFP4 projection on Triton. Its 96 scale rows are legal for the ASM swizzle, and ASM is numerically wrong for M<=64. https://github.com/ROCm/aiter/issues/5931\n\n## Claims\n\n- A weight scale with 96 rows no longer takes the AITER ASM FP4 GEMM.\n- Other layout-legal shapes, including 128 scale rows, still use ASM when AITER reports them as tuned.\n\n## Validation\n\n- `tests/kernels/quantization/test_rocm_mxfp4.py::test_asm_fp4_scale_swizzle_supported_shape_rules` now expects `(96, 8)` to be rejected. That test is gfx950-only and was not run on this machine.\n- The same `{96}` rule is what the measured Qwen3.8-27B MXFP4 TP1 image used.\n\n## Details\n\n`_asm_fp4_scale_swizzle_supported` still requires scale rows divisible by 32 and scale columns divisible by 8. It additionally rejects `_ASM_FP4_FORCE_TRITON_SCALE_ROWS = {96}`.\n\nhttps://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/58139 is the broader form of this bugfix: it rejects every scale-row count below 160. This pull request keeps the measured 96-row exception only.\n\n---\n\n<details>\n<summary> Pull Request Checklist </summary>\n\n- [ ] I used vLLM's `/pr-checklist` skill. (Mandatory for agents, optional for humans).\n- [x] AI assistance was used during the creation of this PR.\n\n- [x] **Design Fit:** Minimizes impact on core components, reuses existing functionality, and justifies added complexity.\n- [x] **Testing and Validation:** Validates the change and ensures any added tests are meaningful and reliable, with CI coverage or documented CI resource constraints and validation performed outside CI.\n- [x] **Code Quality and Style:** Keeps code and comments clear and concise, and updates relevant documentation and examples.\n- [x] **Pull Request Contents:** Includes a brief summary and relevant links, supports claims with evidence, explains root causes and implementation trade-offs, and follows the contributing guide.\n</details>\n\n<!-- codes